          %title Initiality Project - Overview
          %h1#initiality_project__overview Initiality Project - Overview
          %p
            This page is part of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Initiality+Project"} Initiality Project
          %p
            Here is a proposed slate of choices to make regarding type theories, presentations, definitions, and proof structure. (“I” refers to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/9160/initiality-project-plans/"} here
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Semantics"} The semantics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_type_theory"} The type theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#axioms"} Axioms
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#raw_syntax"} Raw syntax
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#bidirectionality"} Bidirectionality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_proof"} The proof
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_partial_interpretation"} The partial interpretation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#totality_of_the_interpretation"} Totality of the interpretation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#loose_ends"} Loose ends
          %h2#Semantics The semantics
          %p
            Of the various ways to describe a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            = succeed "," do
              %em categories with families
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"} Awodey
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.3219"} natural model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            together with two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tm
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Ty
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm,Ty \in [C^{op},Set]
            and a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tm
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ty
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm \to Ty
            that is
            %em algebraically representable
            (or, one might say, “represented”): it is equipped with a
            %em function
            assigning to each morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi y
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ty
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:y \Gamma \to Ty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\in C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            denotes the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo .
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma.A\in C
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+square"} pullback square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo .
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Tm
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                          %mi Γ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ty
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ y(\Gamma.A) &amp; \to &amp; Tm \\ \downarrow &amp;&amp;\downarrow \\ y\Gamma &amp; \xrightarrow{A} &amp; Ty. }
          %p
            This is equivalent to the usual notion of category with families (and thereby also to the usual notion of “category with attributes”, hence also to discrete or full split comprehension categories). But, as noted by Awodey, focusing attention on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tm
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ty
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ty
            as objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            has the advantage that the additional structure corresponding to the rules of a type theory are also naturally formulated in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ty
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ty^2
            denotes the domain of the local exponential
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ty
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Ty
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ty
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Tm
                        %mo →
                        %mi Ty
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Ty\times Ty \to Ty)^{(Tm\to Ty)}
            = succeed "-formation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "-formation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ty
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ty
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ty^2 \to Ty
          %p
            Note also that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ty
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ty^2
            can be described in the internal extensional type theory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            by the context
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi Ty
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi Tm
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ty
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A:Ty, B:Tm(A) \to Ty)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            is a sort of “semantic
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} logical framework
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            A
            %em morphism of categories with families
            is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C\to D
            together with a commutative square in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Tm
                            %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi F
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Tm
                            %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Ty
                            %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi F
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Ty
                            %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ Tm_C &amp; \to &amp; F^*(Tm_D) \\ \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\ Ty_C &amp; \to &amp; F^*(Ty_D) }
          %p
            which strictly respects the chosen pullback squares in an appropriate way. This defines the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CwF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CwF
            in which we hope to construct an initial object.
          %h2#the_type_theory The type theory
          %p
            I suggest to start with a type theory containing only
            = succeed "-types." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
          %h3#axioms Axioms
          %p Note that this type theory is completely empty unless we also assert some axioms. Ideally, I would like the “initiality theorem” to really be a “freeness theorem”, saying that the syntactic category of a type theory with axioms is freely generated by those axioms. Of course, particular axioms can just be added as rules to a theory, but such a general freeness theorem should be parametric over all possible axioms.
          %p
            Since in a dependent type theory, each axiom can involve previous axioms in its type, it seems that the general semantic formulation will have to involve some kind of a “cell complex” in the category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CwF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CwF
            = succeed "-types." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
          %h3#raw_syntax Raw syntax
          %p
            I propose to use syntax with named variables, quotiented by
            = succeed "-equivalence." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %em really
            using them.
          %p If this proposal is adopted, then the raw terms for our simple type theory will be inductively generated by:
          %ul
            %li
              Every variable
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is a term.
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              are terms and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is a variable, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(x:A) B
              is a term.
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,M
              are terms and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is a variable, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo .
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(x:A.B)M
              is a term.
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,M,N
              are terms and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is a variable, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi App
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
              is a term.
          %p
            We define
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(x:A)B
            scopes only over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo .
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda(x:A.B)M
            it scopes only over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            it scopes only over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "-equivalence." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %h3#bidirectionality Bidirectionality
          %p
            Following Streicher, all our terms are fully (or at least “sufficiently”) annotated. For instance, we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            rather than just
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi App
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App(M,N)
          %p
            In particular, this means that given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash t:A
            can be derived for any value of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            that can be deduced syntactically, or
            = succeed "," do
              %em synthesized
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            has any type, then it must have the type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[N/x]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi App
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} App^{(x:A)B}(M,N)
            must also have any type that is judgmentally
            %em equal
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[N/x]
          %p
            Type theorists have a standard technique for distinguishing these two situations, known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bidirectional+typechecking"} bidirectional typechecking
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\vdash t:A
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash t \Rightarrow A
              \: in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              %em synthesizes
              the type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is, if it exists, uniquely determined by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash t \Leftarrow A
              \: in context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              %em checks against
              the type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              are all “inputs” and the only “output” is the truth value of whether the typecheck is valid.
          %p There are various advantages of bidirectional typechecking, including:
          %ol
            %li In at least some cases, it permits a formulation of type theory in which only normal forms exist.
            %li
              In at least some cases, it allows omission of annotations, e.g. an un-annotated abstraction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.M
              cannot synthesize a type, but it can check against a
              = succeed "-type" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(x:A) B
            %li
              In at least some cases, it makes it easier to incorporate
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%B7-expansion"} η-expansion
              into an equality-checking algorithm.
            %li In at least some cases, it makes derivations of judgments unique, or at least canonical.
            %li
              In at least some cases, it isolates the use of judgmental equality during typechecking in one particular “mode-switching” rule:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi t
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≡
                          %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi t
                          %mo ⇐
                          %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash t \Rightarrow A \qquad \Gamma \vdash A\equiv B}{\Gamma \vdash t \Leftarrow B}
          %p
            (Experts, feel free to add more.) It is unclear at present whether any of these advantages are relevant to categorical semantics, but by using a bidirectional framework we are better placed to take advantage of them if they do become relevant. More importantly, it seems to me that the natural structure of the semantic interpretation, as suggested by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Lumsdaine"} Peter Lumsdaine
            (see below), matches the syntactic bidirectional picture quite closely, and it may be clarifying to make that analogy more precise.
          %p
            Since all our terms are fully annotated, all of our formation, introduction, and elimination rules can synthesize their types. However, their
            %em premises
            usually involve
            %em checking
            their subterms against appropriate types, so the above mode-switching rule gets applied a lot (it is the only way, in our system, to derive a checking judgment). This might be an issue to want to optimize away in an implementation, but for our semantic purposes it seems irrelevant.
          %p
            For instance, here are the formation, introduction, and elimination rules for
            = succeed "-types:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A \, type \qquad \Gamma, x:A \vdash B\,type}{\Gamma \vdash \Pi(x:A) B \, type}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi N
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msup
                        %mi App
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A \, type \qquad \Gamma, x:A \vdash B\,type \qquad \Gamma \vdash M \Leftarrow \Pi(x:A) B \qquad \Gamma \vdash N \Leftarrow A}{\Gamma \vdash App^{(x:A)B}(M,N) \Rightarrow B[N/x]}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi λ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo .
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A \, type \qquad \Gamma,x:A \vdash B \,type \qquad \Gamma,x:A \vdash M \Leftarrow B}{\Gamma \vdash \lambda(x:A.B)M \Rightarrow \Pi(x:A) B}
          %p
            (Note that the “is a type” judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\,type
            is not bidirectional. With Russell universes we could consider making it so.)
          %p The “hypothesis” rule is that a variable in the context synthesizes its assumed type:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi A
                      %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{(x:A) \in \Gamma}{\Gamma \vdash x \Rightarrow A.}
          %p
            In implementations of bidirectional typechecking, the equality judgments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi s
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash s\equiv t : A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A \equiv B \, type
            are sometimes made bidirectional as well. But at the moment, I don’t think there will be is any benefit to us in doing likewise. In particular, we would like our setup to be general enough to encompass type theories whose judgmental equality is “poorly behaved”, e.g. undecidable, such as those with an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} equality reflection rule
          %p
            Streicher also includes a basic judgment for equality of
            = succeed "." do
              %em contexts
            %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/8904/a-communal-proof-of-an-initiality-theorem/?Focus=71254#Comment_71254"} suggests
            that that isn’t necessary, because
          %blockquote
            %p
              any contexts that
              %em would
              be judgementally equal in the system with that judgement still end up canonically end up judgementally isomorphic, by the substitution that consists of not renaming variables.
          %p I’m inclined to follow Peter’s suggestion here, but am open to counterarguments.
          %p Streicher also includes a number of explicit primitive rules involving things like validity of contexts and type-preservation by substitution. I’m not sure whether these are necessary or not; it seems that maybe by presenting our type theories in “the usual way” we should always be able to ensure that substitution is admissible, even for type theories that are “ill-behaved” in other ways (such as equality reflection). But maybe there are good reasons to include these rules explicitly?
          %h2#the_proof The proof
          %p
            The following proposal for the inductive structure of the argument is based on Streicher’s original approach, with a modification suggested by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/8904/a-communal-proof-of-an-initiality-theorem/?Focus=71254#Comment_71254"} Peter Lumsdaine
          %h3#the_partial_interpretation The partial interpretation
          %p
            Given a finite set of variables
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Var
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \{x_1,\dots,x_n\} \subseteq Var
            %em presheaf of environments
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Tm
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm^V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of variables, the following partial morphisms of presheaves.
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                    %mi t
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                      %mi type
                      %mi V
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Tm
                      %mi V
                    %mo ⇀
                    %mi Ty
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \llbracket t \rrbracket ^V_{type} : Tm^V \rightharpoonup Ty
              \.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                    %mi t
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi V
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Tm
                      %mi V
                    %mo ⇀
                    %mi Tm
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \llbracket t \rrbracket ^V_{\Rightarrow} : Tm^V \rightharpoonup Tm
              \.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                    %mi t
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi V
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Tm
                      %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Ty
                    %mo ⇀
                    %mi Tm
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \llbracket t \rrbracket ^V_{\Leftarrow} : Tm^V \times Ty \rightharpoonup Tm
              such that the composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Tm
                      %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Ty
                    %mo ⇀
                    %mi Tm
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ty
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm^V \times Ty \rightharpoonup Tm \to Ty
              is the second projection.
          %p
            In fact, the third of these is not actually inductive at all: it is simply a semantic counterpart of the bidirectional mode-switching judgment. We define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                  %mi t
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \llbracket t \rrbracket ^V_{\Leftarrow}
            to be the domain restriction of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Tm
                    %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ty
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pr
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi Tm
                    %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo ⇀
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                      %mi t
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi V
                  %mi Tm
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm^V \times Ty \xrightarrow{pr_1} Tm^V \overset{\llbracket t \rrbracket ^V_{\Rightarrow}}{\rightharpoonup} Tm
          %p to the equalizer of
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Tm
                    %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ty
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pr
                        %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi Tm
                    %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo ⇀
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟦
                      %mi t
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⟧
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi V
                  %mi Tm
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ty
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm^V \times Ty \xrightarrow{pr_1} Tm^V \overset{\llbracket t \rrbracket ^V_{\Rightarrow}}{\rightharpoonup} Tm \to Ty
          %p
            and the restriction of the second projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Tm
                    %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ty
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm^V \times Ty
            to its domain.
          %p
            The other inductive clauses of the partial interpretation should precisely mirror the bidirectional rules of our type theory, using recursive calls to the above three operations as counterparts of the three syntactic judgments
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\,type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash t\Rightarrow A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash t\Leftarrow A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %p
            This suggestion incorporates the innovation proposed by
            %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/8904/a-communal-proof-of-an-initiality-theorem/?Focus=71254#Comment_71254"} Peter
            over Streicher’s proof of taking the interpretation of the context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            as an
            %em input
            to the partial intepretation functions rather than an output (see in particular his point (7)). The elements of the presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Tm
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm^V
            at some object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in C
            are what Peter calls “environments” for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            contain all free variables occurring in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %p
            I have also reformulated the interpretation function to “put naturality into the goals of the original induction” as suggested by Peter in his point (8), by directly defining partial morphisms in the “semantic logical framework”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},Set]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Semantics"} above
          %h3#totality_of_the_interpretation Totality of the interpretation
          %p Although the partial interpretation functions sketched above incorporate a number of “tweaks” to make it more pleasing, in fact it was already the short part: Streicher does it in only 5 pages. (His pages are very short, so this isn’t actually very much, plus his type theory is more complicated.) The long part is proving that these interpretation functions are total on derivable judgments; this takes Streicher 40 pages.
          %p The outline should be the same as in Streicher’s proof and Peter’s proposal:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Prove by induction on raw terms that the partial interpretation functions take syntactic substitution (including weakening) to semantic substitution (restriction in the presheaves
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Tm
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tm
                and
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ty
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ty
            %li
              %p Prove by induction on derivations that the partial interpretation functions are total on derivable judgments. This takes Streicher 20 pages.
          %h3#loose_ends Loose ends
          %p To complete the proof of initiality after this, it remains to:
          %ol
            %li
              %p Construct the term model. This takes Streicher 12 pages.
            %li
              %p
                Show that the above interpretation assembles into a morphism in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CwF
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CwF
                from the term model to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p Show that this morphism is unique. Streicher doesn’t actually do this either.
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/Initiality+Project"} Initiality Project
